sentences.text:
The first clause operates where the woman is in possession of her senses and, therefore, capable of consenting but the act is done against her will and the second where it is done without her consent;
the third, fourth and fifth when there is consent but it is not such a consent as excuses the offender,
because it is obtained by putting her, or any person in whom she is interested, in fear of death or of hurt. The expression "against her 'will' " means that the act must have been done in spite of the opposition of the woman.
